-- Shooter — the extremes. This one exists to be hard on the hardware.
--
--   * a row of enemies at the same y, which is the per-scanline sprite limit's
--     worst case: eight on a NES or a Master System, ten on a Game Boy, and
--     anything past that simply does not draw
--   * a fast bullet against thin targets, close to the tunnelling threshold
--   * a magazine of bullets recycled rather than spawned
--   * two independent level rules driving the same object (the enemy line
--     advances and sways)

start play

scene play

create object ship (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, speed 60vw, sprite hero.svg)
create object shot (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, speed 70vh, sprite shot.svg)
create object alien (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, speed 8vw, sprite alien.svg)

create ship player in play (x centerx, y screenheight - 2)

-- Nine aliens on one row. On a Game Boy that is nine of the ten sprites the
-- hardware will draw on that scanline; add the player's shot passing through and
-- the eleventh vanishes. The simulator reports the peak as it runs.
create alien a1 in play (x 4vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a2 in play (x 14vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a3 in play (x 24vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a4 in play (x 34vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a5 in play (x 44vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a6 in play (x 54vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a7 in play (x 64vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a8 in play (x 74vw, y 3, direction east)
create alien a9 in play (x 84vw, y 3, direction east)

-- One shot, reused, and now it cannot be re-fired mid-flight: the `if` guard on
-- the firing rule reads the shot's own state.
create shot s1 in play (x centerx, y screenheight - 3, visible 0, direction north)

create number score in play (value 0, x 1, y 1)

control player left (xdirection -1) on hold
control player right (xdirection 1) on hold

when player hits screenleft, screenright then xdirection as 0

-- Firing puts the shot at the ship and makes it solid. The `if` guard is what
-- makes this a *reload* rather than a restart: press while the shot is in flight
-- and nothing happens, because the guard reads its state at the instant the
-- button edge arrives.
when a pressed if s1.visible = 0 then (s1.visible, s1.x, s1.y) as (1, player.x, player.y - 1)

when s1 hits screentop then s1.visible as 0

when shot hits alien then (alien.visible, shot.visible, score.value) as (0, 0, score.value + 1)

-- The line sways by bouncing off the walls. A `hits` rule can target the *class*
-- — every alien at once — because collision binds the object that collided. A
-- level rule cannot: `when always (alien.xdirection)` has no subject to bind, so
-- driving a whole class from a condition is not expressible today. Another
-- finding this file is here to produce.
when alien hits screenleft, screenright then xdirection as flip
